  "query_preview": "..\n\nA cavalcade of wet storm systems with ties to subtropical \nmoisture will take aim at the Pacific NW through the period. \nMountain areas across Northwest WA and Southwest British \nColumbia including the Olympics and North Cascades will receive \nthe bulk of the precipitation, but periods of light to moderate \nprecipitation will at times make it over to the east slopes of \nthe Cascades and higher terrain across Northeast WA and North \nID. Upper-level flow out of the west/southwest will favor rain \nshadowing across lowland areas of Central and Eastern WA, \nresulting in minimal rain for these areas.\n\nBetween each round of precipitation, there will be short- lived\nbreaks allowing for some drying. Ensemble clusters are in good \nagreement with the general, active weather pattern across the \nPacific Northwest. However, some uncertainty remains as to how \nmuch moderate to heavy rain will slop over the Cascade crest, \nimpacting the Cascade burn scars and creating concern for debris\nflows with soils becoming increasingly saturated.\n\nSaturday night into Sunday: The first weather system arrives \ntonight. Mid and high level clouds are spreading across the \nInland NW from west to east this afternoon as a cold front \napproaches. The front will move into the Cascades overnight, \nthen will reach the Inland NW by Sunday morning. In addition to \nbands of moderate rain, the front will bring gusty westerly \nwinds. Winds will start ramping up between 8pm and midnight \ntonight and will remain gusty through Sunday afternoon. \nAccording to the National Blend of Models, chances for wind \ngusts greater than 30 mph range from 60% to nearly 100% with the\nhighest chances across the Columbia Basin, Waterville Plateau, \nand into the Spokane/Coeur d'Alene area. For gusts greater than \n40 mph, chances range from 40% to 60% across the Columbia Basin,\nWaterville Plateau, and West Plains. Elsewhere, chances are \ngenerally less than 20%.\n\nTravelers heading over the Cascade passes will mainly encounter \nrain through this evening. Following the cold front passage \ntonight, rain showers will transition to snow showers for \nStevens and Washington Pass. While Stevens Pass is not expected \nto receive impactful snow, there is potential for 1-2 inches if\nthe Puget Sound Convergence Zone sets up over the pass. For\nWashington Pass, 3-4 inches are forecast. \n\nElsewhere outside of the highest Cascade passes, precipitation\nwill be in the form of rain over the course of the event. Expect\nrainfall amounts of 1.00-1.50 inches along the immediate \nCascade crest and 0.50-0.75 inches along the east slopes. For \nmost of Central and Eastern WA and North ID, rainfall amounts \nwill range from a couple hundredths of an inch up to 0.25\ninches. The highest intensity rain for the Cascades will come \nright along the cold front overnight. \n\nMonday and Tuesday: Rain will taper off Sunday afternoon and \nevening as a transient ridge of high pressure sets up over the \nregion, keeping conditions mainly dry through Monday afternoon \naside from a few spotty, very light showers. The next round of \nsteady precipitation will come with a warm front Monday evening \nthrough Tuesday. This front will bring the next chance for \nanother 0.10 to 0.50 inches over the Cascades and 0.10 to 0.25 \ninches over Eastern WA and North ID. \n\nWednesday through Friday: There is moderate confidence for \nanother ridge to amplify over the region Wednesday into Friday,\ndelivering the next period of dry conditions. Following a couple\nrounds of rainfall, it is almost a guarantee that there will be\nareas of fog and low stratus with this ridge. /Fewkes",
